From 7785bb382453ac5b662905bda3926ba977fa17ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josh Hoffer <jhoffer@sansorgan.es>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 00:39:43 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] key: add the attestation key as an object of the token

CKM_TPM2_CERTIFY names its signer by a persistent TPM handle, so the
caller must have made a key with the TPM tools and needs access to the TPM
to have one. Make the attestation key an ordinary key pair of the token.

CKM_TPM2_AK_PAIR_GEN (C_GenerateKeyPair, no parameter) makes a restricted
ECDSA signing key, fixed to the TPM, with the hash of its curve (P-256,
P-384, P-521). Its private key has CKA_SIGN false and an empty
CKA_ALLOWED_MECHANISMS, so C_SignInit refuses it in the module: a
restricted key would fail in the TPM, which wants a hashcheck ticket.
Templates asking for a usage attribute true, or an extractable key, are
CKR_TEMPLATE_INCONSISTENT.

CKM_TPM2_CERTIFY_OBJECT is CKM_TPM2_CERTIFY signed by such a key, named by
its object handle (CK_TPM2_CERTIFY_OBJECT_PARAMS). The key has an auth
value of the module's, which it proves in an HMAC session of its own:
the TPM takes no session twice in a command, and a password session
would put the auth value on the wire. Any other signer is
CKR_MECHANISM_PARAM_INVALID.

Test: pkcs-attest-key.int generates keys on two curves, checks the
attributes and the TPM public area, certifies private and secret keys and
verifies the statements with OpenSSL, checks the refusals, and that the key
survives C_Finalize/C_Initialize. docs/VENDOR.md describes the interface.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Josh Hoffer <jhoffer@sansorgan.es>
